1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,800
Fold a sheet of paper in half forty two

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,040
times, and mathematically, it would be

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:07,280
thicker than the distance to the Moon.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,400
Sounds impossible?

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,760
The math checks out.

6
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,000
Every fold doubles the thickness.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,080
Here's the math, and why engineers take

8
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,600
doubling seriously.

9
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,840
Let's follow the folds, one doubling at a

10
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:18,390
time.

11
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,080
Start with an ordinary sheet of printer

12
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,160
paper. It's about a tenth of a millimeter

13
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,640
thick. Fold it in half once, and now you

14
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:25,520
have two layers.

15
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,680
Fold again, and you have four layers.

16
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:29,120
Again, eight.

17
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,360
Every fold doubles the number of layers,

18
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,520
so every fold doubles the thickness.

19
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,840
In this ideal version, we assume the paper

20
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,000
never gets squashed, so every layer stays

21
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,920
a tenth of a millimeter thick.

22
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,280
Let's write it down.

23
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,160
Zero folds, zero point one millimeters.

24
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,160
One fold, zero point two.

25
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:46,337
Then zero point four, zero point eight,

26
00:00:46,347 --> 00:00:49,920
one point six, three point

27
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:52,240
two, and six point four.

28
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:53,910
At fold seven, the stack is twelve point

29
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,080
eight millimeters, thicker than most

30
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,150
phones. At fold ten, it's one hundred and

31
00:00:58,160 --> 00:00:59,600
two millimeters.

32
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:01,330
That's about ten centimeters, the height

33
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:02,370
of a coffee mug.

34
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:04,770
Ten folds made it about a thousand times

35
00:01:04,770 --> 00:01:05,570
thicker.

36
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:07,410
Here's the pattern as a formula.

37
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:09,250
Call the starting thickness t.

38
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,610
After n folds, the thickness T is t times

39
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:14,210
two to the n.

40
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:15,970
Now notice something handy.

41
00:01:15,970 --> 00:01:17,320
Two to the ten is one thousand and twenty

42
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:20,770
four. That's almost exactly a thousand.

43
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:22,770
So every ten folds multiplies the

44
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:24,530
thickness by about a thousand.

45
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:26,610
Ten folds, about ten centimeters.

46
00:01:26,610 --> 00:01:28,690
Twenty folds, about a hundred meters.

47
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:31,410
Thirty folds, about a hundred kilometers.

48
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:33,570
Now compare two ways to grow.

49
00:01:33,570 --> 00:01:35,330
Both start with one sheet.

50
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:38,050
The blue line adds one new sheet at every

51
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,210
step. That's linear growth.

52
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:42,690
The red curve doubles the sheets at every

53
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,850
step. That's exponential growth.

54
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:46,850
For the first few steps, they look almost

55
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,490
the same. But after ten steps, the blue

56
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:51,400
stack has eleven sheets, just one point

57
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:53,490
one millimeters thick.

58
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:55,000
The red stack has one thousand and twenty

59
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:58,050
four sheets, over a hundred

60
00:01:58,050 --> 00:02:00,690
millimeters. Adding is slow and steady.

61
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:02,960
Doubling runs away.

62
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:05,440
Keep folding, and switch the scale to

63
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:08,000
kilometers. For about twenty folds, the

64
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,600
curve hugs the floor.

65
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,200
By then the stack is about a hundred

66
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:12,960
meters, as tall as a thirty storey

67
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,360
building. Three folds later, it's taller

68
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:17,440
than the Burj Khalifa, the tallest

69
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,560
building in the world.

70
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,880
At fold twenty seven, it's thirteen

71
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,640
kilometers. That's higher than Mount

72
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:25,120
Everest, and higher than airliners fly.

73
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:26,710
At fold thirty, it's about a hundred and

74
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:28,320
seven kilometers.

75
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,560
That's past the Kármán line, the commonly

76
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,240
used boundary of space.

77
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,160
Now picture the stack in thousands of

78
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,830
kilometers. The Moon is about three

79
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,520
hundred and eighty four thousand

80
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:38,720
kilometers away.

81
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,726
At fold forty, the stack is about a

82
00:02:40,736 --> 00:02:42,960
hundred and ten thousand kilometers.

83
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:44,320
At fold forty one, two hundred and twenty

84
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:47,600
thousand. Still only partway there.

85
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,800
Then fold forty two doubles it again, to

86
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,560
about four hundred and forty thousand

87
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,960
kilometers. You've passed the Moon.

88
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:56,880
And here's the astonishing part.

89
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,977
The final fold alone added more than two

90
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:01,360
hundred thousand kilometers, as much

91
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:03,760
thickness as all forty one previous folds

92
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:04,480
combined.

93
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:06,550
We can also run it backwards.

94
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:08,550
How many folds does it take to reach the

95
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:12,390
Moon? Set t times two to the n equal to

96
00:03:12,390 --> 00:03:13,090
the distance.

97
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:15,030
Put both in meters, and divide.

98
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:17,740
That gives two to the n equals about three

99
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:21,110
point eight four times ten to the twelve.

100
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:23,110
To solve for an exponent, you use a

101
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:25,713
logarithm. Log base two of that number is

102
00:03:25,723 --> 00:03:27,430
forty one point eight.

103
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,590
You can't make eight tenths of a fold, so

104
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:31,990
round up. Forty two folds.

105
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:34,540
Why can't you actually fold paper forty

106
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:37,110
two times? Because each fold makes the

107
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:39,190
stack thicker while reducing the area

108
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:40,870
available for the next fold.

109
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:43,030
After seven folds, an ordinary sheet could

110
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:44,950
already be more than a centimeter thick,

111
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,430
with only a tiny footprint remaining.

112
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:49,910
At that point, bending the growing stack

113
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:51,830
becomes extremely difficult.

114
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:53,910
That's why most people can only manage

115
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:56,070
around six or seven folds with ordinary

116
00:03:56,070 --> 00:03:57,510
printer paper.

117
00:03:57,510 --> 00:03:59,590
For years, people commonly believed you

118
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:01,270
couldn't fold paper in half more than

119
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:03,830
seven times. Then in two thousand and one,

120
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:05,510
a California high school student named

121
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:07,510
Britney Gallivan was challenged by her

122
00:04:07,510 --> 00:04:09,030
math teacher to fold something in half

123
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,280
twelve times for extra credit.

124
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,960
In January two thousand and two, she

125
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,923
folded a strip of toilet paper about one

126
00:04:14,933 --> 00:04:18,080
point two kilometers long in half twelve

127
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:20,400
times, all in the same direction.

128
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:30,720
Gallivan also derived a formula for the

129
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,560
minimum length of paper needed when every

130
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:35,920
fold goes in

131
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,120
the same direction.

132
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:42,640
The equation shows that

133
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,240
the required length grows roughly fourfold

134
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,400
with every additional fold.

135
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,320
For forty two

136
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,000
folds of paper one tenth of a

137
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,920
millimeter thick, the theoretical length

138
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,341
would be more than ten to the twenty one

139
00:05:11,351 --> 00:05:11,430
meters. That's approximately one hundred

140
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:13,550
thousand light years, comparable to

141
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:17,087
the diameter of the Milky Way.

142
00:05:17,097 --> 00:05:17,247
Of course, this is a mathematical

143
00:05:17,257 --> 00:05:17,380
extrapolation, not something we could

144
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:17,433
physically build.

145
00:05:17,443 --> 00:05:17,593
This isn't just a party trick.

146
00:05:17,603 --> 00:05:17,780
Engineers run into doubling all the time.

147
00:05:17,790 --> 00:05:19,082
For decades, the number of transistors on

148
00:05:19,092 --> 00:05:19,133
a computer chip doubled roughly every two

149
00:05:19,143 --> 00:05:19,240
years. That's Moore's law, an industry

150
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:19,380
trend rather than a law of physics.

151
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:22,190
In a nuclear reactor, neutrons released

152
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:25,807
when an atom splits can go on to split

153
00:05:25,817 --> 00:05:28,073
more atoms, so the chain reaction has to

154
00:05:28,083 --> 00:05:28,660
be carefully controlled.

155
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:28,776
And money growing at seven percent a year,

156
00:05:28,786 --> 00:05:29,630
with compound interest, doubles in a

157
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:36,830
little over ten years.

158
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,700
So here's the one thing to remember.

159
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:40,580
Ten doublings is about a thousand times.

160
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:43,950
Forty two doublings turn a tenth of a

161
00:05:43,950 --> 00:05:46,350
millimeter into four hundred and forty

162
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:46,820
thousand kilometers.

163
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:50,750
That's how a sheet of paper, at least

164
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:52,750
on paper, reaches the

165
00:06:00,670 --> 00:06:01,710
Moon.

166
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:03,540
If you enjoyed this one, please give it a

167
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:07,390
like and subscribe for more everyday

168
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:08,590
engineering. Thanks for watching!
